+# FAA Pittsburgh radar transfer labels and the NTSB study directory
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review. B129 / PD01–PD04 and REC01 / REC03 acquisition. Three FAA inventories list the same Pittsburgh radar filename under the same date. The National Archives catalog lists that filename on the FAA's **AWA-715** disc. The NTSB's study lists a Pittsburgh input under a different name. The FAA records give a 2001 context for the **9/21** listing and a stronger inventory-to-catalog match. The original transmittal, NTSB receipt and study-file lineage remain open. [FAA disc finding aid](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid-96-107#:~:text=Radar%20Data%20Released%20to%20NTSB) · [NTSB reading room, folder 32](https://securefoia.ntsb.gov/app/FolderInformation.aspx?id=32&ispal=F)
+
+## FAA inventories and the study input
+
+| Record and locator | Pittsburgh observation |
+| --- | --- |
+| AWA-606, physical/printed p. 5, radar table row 4 | Facility `PIT`, time period blank, filename `PIT UAL93.txt`, extraction `CDR`, `To NTSB` `9/21`. Footer `9/27 1400`. |
+| AEA-2353, physical p. 7 / printed p. 6, radar table row 4 | The same five fields. Footer `9/25 1530`. Cover p. 1 dated **September 26, 2001**. |
+| AEA-2354, physical p. 7 / printed p. 6, radar table row 4 | The same five fields. Footer partially cropped. Cover p. 1 dated **October 3, 2001**. |
+| AWA-715, finding aid raw HTML rows 12206 and 12323 | Disc description `Radar Data Released to NTSB 9/21`. Item **7596916** ends in `PIT UAL93.txt`. The catalog object size field is **13,028,310** bytes. |
+| NTSB Recorded Radar Data Study, physical/printed p. 9 | Under the United 93 directory the image reads `ual93 PIT Tracon 1300 to 1440.txt` with source `FAA`. The sorted extraction at line 30 starts `ua193`. Keep both readings. |
+| FAA accession inventory, physical p. 264 sorted line 26 | **AEA#861** identifies a Pittsburgh CDR compact disc with **13:30:02–14:30:09 UTC**. This is a separate label with a different written interval from the study filename. |
+
+[AWA-606](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7596153) · [AEA-2353](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7600802) · [AEA-2354](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34912007) · [AWA-715 item](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7596916) · [NTSB study, p. 9](https://www.ntsb.gov/about/Documents/Rec_Radar_Data_Study_all_aircraft.pdf#page=9) · [FAA accession inventory](https://www.archives.gov/files/faa-inventory.pdf#page=264)
+
+The FAA inventories share field order and values and may derive from one maintained list. Their common filename is the same as the literal suffix in the catalog. The NTSB report's filename differs. An exact case-insensitive filename screen of the 12 AWA-715 UA93 names against the study's pp. 9–10 extracted text yielded **zero** matches. The page images were inspected for the Pittsburgh entry and the adjacent directory list. The screen leaves renaming, extraction, combination and an independent input as untested possibilities. The 13,028,310-byte catalog value was read from metadata. No file bytes, record extents or time alignment were tested in this unit. The native AWA-715 release record and the NTSB study CD remain separate targets.
+
+### Other fields worth carrying forward
+
+The selected voice rows on **AEA-2353 p. 7** give EWR **1204–1226 GC** and **1235–1247 LC**, with a blank tape field, `Partial` transcript, `9/13` received and `9/21` to NTSB. **AWA-606 p. 5** and **AEA-2354 p. 7** give `Tape`, `Partial`, `9/13 &26` and `9/27` for both rows. Preserve the distinct date and tape fields. The signed or logged receipt and actual tape and transcript identifiers would distinguish an additional tape shipment from a revision of the same transfer entry. The earlier `9/25 1530` sheet also lists `9/26` in other To NTSB cells. That is a reason to seek actual dispatch and receipt before treating all values as completed transfers. [Earlier inventory](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7600802) · [Later inventory](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34912007)
+
+## Two original alert scans and a separate route slip
+
+| Catalog and scan | What the scan records |
+| --- | --- |
+| **AWA-832 / 34913675** | The finding aid description gives **Release of FAA Radar Data to USAF or Other Gov Org**. Its linked scan instead shows **Data and Voice Extraction (Update)**, **09/14/2001**, serial **101362**, physical pp. 1–2. |
+| **AWA-833 / 34913677** | The finding aid description gives **Data and Voice Extraction Update**. Its linked scan instead shows **Release of FAA Radar Data to USAF or Other Gov Org**, **09/17/2001**, serial **101381**, physical p. 1. |
+| **ZOB-189 / 7597315** | The **October 23, 2001** route slip, physical p. 1 sorted lines 7, 10 and 19–20, describes sending Indianapolis ARTCC support data to Cleveland for **Formal Accident # ZOB-ARTCC-287 (UAL93)**. The returned PDF has one page and contains no embedded attachments. The accompanying support data remain open. |
+
+Preserve the catalog descriptions, object filenames and visible titles separately. The visible scans have different dates, page counts and serials. The 9/14 alert names the **AXX Region Data and Voice Extractions** spreadsheet and calls for documenting extraction details, security, requesting organization and recipient. It separately gives a 0900Z–2100Z September 11 Host SAR duplication window and a midnight-to-midnight data/voice securing window in the update. The 9/17 alert describes regional routing through **AXX-400** to **NOCC** and records the First Air Force's written-request instruction. It does not supply an AWA-715 decision, approval or completed transaction. The October route slip is an intra-FAA transfer with different date and parties from the NTSB referral. [AWA-832](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34913675) · [AWA-833](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/34913677) · [ZOB-189](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/7597315)
+
+## Verification and open inputs
+
+Acquired November 10, 2026 UTC. Verified seven saved response hashes, three PDF catalog-object bindings, the Pittsburgh metadata, selected inventory image readings and the exact-name screen. The alert date, title and serial fields were read from page images. Compared the NTSB reading-room edition's pp. 9–10 with the held FAA catalog rows. The study's official direct PDF and reading-room version are distinct previously pinned files. This unit uses the reading-room edition for the page image and text screen. No media processing or recorder-clock test was performed.
+
+- [ ] **REC01 / REC03 / PD01.** Obtain AWA-715's dated outgoing cover and receipt, the completed Pittsburgh extraction spreadsheet and the PIT-36 custody document. Compare the exact archived `PIT UAL93.txt` and NTSB `ual93 PIT Tracon 1300 to 1440.txt` using hashes, record headers, transformations and coverage if their native files become available. Retain AEA#861 separately until an item-level join is made.
+- [ ] Obtain the actual ZOB-189 attachment and identify any relationship to the AWA-715 ZOB files. Seek FAA finding-aid preparation and file-association records for the AWA-832 and AWA-833 description reversal, and original NOCC regional and transfer records.
+- [ ] Keep the **2006005841** referral CD and return/receipt records open. The corrected May 2003 FAA answer and dated receipt, signed administrative decision, recommendation outcomes and case-specific procedure remain outstanding. [Case queue](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/055ef54a857b46846d0038109bab7e108970f03a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-postreport-disposition-2026-10-27/README.md)
